WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.230
Once you've had a villain who can kill half
the universe with a snap of his fingers, what

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.620
chance does any other villain have of being
that scary or threatening?

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.880
Let's take a look at some villains from Marvel
Comics who can give Thanos a run for his money

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.200
when it comes to being frightening.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.439
Galactus isn’t necessarily evil, but he’s
definitely terrifying.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.840
He’s an unstoppable force of nature, and
while he doesn’t actually want to hurt any

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.630
of the trillions of people whose lives he’s
ended over the years, that’s not because

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.060
he has any affection for them.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.440
It’s because he just doesn’t care about
them at all.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:35.260
"When the great hunger is upon me, all creatures
are enemies!"

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.920
Galactus first arrived in Fantastic Four #48,
by Stan Lee and Jack Kirby, kicking off decades

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.420
of his status as the definitive cosmic threat
in Marvel Comics.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.739
He’s a survivor of the universe before ours,
reborn in the Big Bang as the devourer of

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:54.239
worlds, a creature seemingly beyond morality
who is driven by his endless hunger to eat

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:55.400
entire planets.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.559
While he’s been defeated every time he’s
come to Earth in the past, including once

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:04.250
when he was confronted with the souls of every
living creature he’d devoured in his billion-year

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:07.360
life, his very existence is downright Lovecraftian.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:12.040
For all its colorful superheroes, the Marvel
Universe also features a galactic force to

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:17.000
whom all of humanity is completely insignificant,
and who might show up to end life on Earth

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.240
whenever he gets peckish.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.120
When Annihilus was first introduced in the
pages of Fantastic Four, he was called “the

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.440
Living Death that Walks,” and in the years
since, he’s more than lived up to that title.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:32.040
Thanos might’ve killed half the universe,
and Galactus might devour planets, but they

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.480
at least see what they’re doing as necessary.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:39.240
Annihilus, on the other hand, doesn’t just
pillage his way across the galaxy, he enjoys

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.000
every second of it.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.040
At heart, Annihilus is an insect, and as the
ruler of the chaotic, interdimensional Negative

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.220
Zone, he’s got an entire swarm of space
locusts at his command ready to consume everything

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:50.880
in their path.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:56.130
That’s exactly what happened in Marvel’s
Annihilation crossover, where he led an insectoid

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.320
armada to destroy entire planets.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.580
He’s incapable of pity or remorse, and would
casually snuff out billions of lives just

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:04.740
to expand his empire.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:10.440
The Marvel Universe is full of unsettling
monstrosities, and compared to them, an overgrown

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.680
octopus with a gigantic eye might not seem
that scary.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:16.680
The thing is, that’s not really Shuma-Gorath.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.880
It’s just the way it appears in the physical
world.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:24.660
In reality, well, its reality, anyway, it’s
part of an extra-dimensional race of beings

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:28.800
known as the Many-Angled Ones who ruled Earth
as gods during prehistory.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:34.000
It was cast down by Crom, the uncaring warrior
god worshipped by Conan the Barbarian, but

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:36.740
it’s been trying to find its way back to
Earth ever since.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:40.080
"I will come for your dimension next"

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.000
As Marvel’s attempt to bring some H.P.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:46.420
Lovecraft-style horror to the comics page,
Shuma-Gorath was terrifying from its very

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.510
first appearance, where it forced Doctor Strange
to kill his own teacher in order to stop the

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:52.200
evil possessing him from within.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:57.040
There have been evil gods in the Marvel Universe
for as long as Loki’s been around, but Shuma-Gorath

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.319
was the first one to be inhuman and incomprehensible,
which makes it way scarier.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.790
As a creature who exists entirely on the astral
plane, in the realm of thoughts, the Shadow

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:08.440
King has no body of his own.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:12.740
He can, however, take over anyone else’s
body, and consume their personality.

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:16.940
When that happens, we're not just talking
about the kind of mind control that's common

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:18.100
in superhero stories.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.950
The Shadow King wants to experience the physical
world however he can, spending years in a

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:28.580
body, using it to live out his every hedonistic
desire and ruining the body in the process.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:33.180
One of the most horrifying examples of his
parasitic use of his hosts comes in the pages

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.740
of New Mutants, when he took over Karma and
twisted her body into something monstrous

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.360
from an indulgence in carnal pleasures.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.700
More recently, he’s been used as the primary
villain on the TV series Legion, which depicts

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.320
him as the chameleon-like creature of corruption
that he's always been in the comics.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:54.700
Killgrave, also known as the Purple Man, isn't
a god, or a cosmic entity, or a non-corporeal

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:55.560
psychic being.

00:03:56.050 --> 00:04:00.540
He's just a man with one of the most simple
super-powers imaginable: he can make anyone

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:01.800
do anything he wants.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.340
His slightest suggestion is an irresistible
command.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.040
If he tells someone to stop breathing, they'll
suffocate without drawing another breath.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.040
He can't even turn his power on and off.

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:16.440
When he was first created, he was just another
mind-controlling supervillain, but over the

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:20.959
years, his ability to effortlessly control
everyone around him twisted his mind into

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.420
something truly horrifying.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:27.780
When he was brought back to comics as part
of Jessica Jones' backstory, his true terrifying

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:32.900
potential was on display, with his ability
to rob victims of their own will and autonomy

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:36.100
combined with the very real-world horror of
sexual assault.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.180
Comics creators borrow ideas from other pieces
of pop culture all the time, and the Brood

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.680
might be one of the best examples.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:48.700
Created in the pages of Uncanny X-Men in 1982,
they’re pretty much just the Marvel Universe

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:52.460
equivalent of the creature from Ridley Scott’s
1979 film Alien.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:57.160
In the interest of doing something slightly
different from that film, though, Chris Claremont

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:01.460
and Dave Cockrum created an even more viscerally
frightening body horror concept.

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:05.860
While the xenomorph from Alien implants an
egg that bursts out of a human body and kills

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.400
the host, the Brood reproduce in another way.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:13.020
Their eggs don’t burst out, they take over
their victims’ bodies and transform them

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:13.880
into new Brood.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:18.260
The host is still trapped in there while their
new form uses their memories and knowledge

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:20.100
to more effectively conquer the galaxy.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:24.840
In other words, the Brood are the answer to
the question “what if xenomorphs were also

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:28.780
zombies, and also trapped you in a mutated
body that you couldn’t control?”

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:31.940
Fear comes in many shapes, and these guys
are at least three of them.

